Keep the VA-API hwaccel across an HEVC SPS change that does not affect it

hevc activates a new SPS at an IRAP whose PPS points at an SPS object other
than the active one (hevcdec.c:3217), and then always runs get_format()
(hevcdec.c:3260). ff_get_format() begins with ff_hwaccel_uninit()
(decode.c:1256): ff_vaapi_decode_uninit() destroys the VA decode context and
config (vaapi_decode.c:744, 752), the hwaccel private data is freed and
avctx->hw_frames_ctx is dropped (decode.c:1219-1226). hwaccel_init()
(decode.c:1341) then builds a new frames context and a new VA context
(vaapi_decode.c:701-720). This happens even when the new SPS differs only in
fields the hwaccel never looks at.

Stranger Things Blu-ray remuxes (HEVC Main10 4K, Dolby Vision profile 7)
switch SPS/PPS at the logo -> programme IRAP and again before the closing.
The SPS differs only in HRD bit_rate/cpb values (and on one episode
bit_rate_scale/cpb_size_scale) and max_transform_hierarchy_depth_inter/intra,
the PPS in transform_skip_enabled_flag. Measured on the production files (CPU
only) at the splices that matter - S05E02 start 11.053s, S05E03 end
3975.305s, S05E04 end 5016.511s:
- the keyframe is IDR_N_LP with in-band VPS, SPS and PPS, and there are no
  RASL pictures;
- the VPS parses identically everywhere, so it is byte-identical and its
  object is kept (ps.c:797-800);
- both SPS variants are general_profile_idc 2 (Main 10), 3840x2160,
  bit_depth_luma_minus8 2, chroma 4:2:0.
So every keep condition below holds exactly where production crashed.

Destroying the context there has two costs:
- A new hw_frames_ctx reaches fftools, which rebuilds the filter graph
  ("Reconfiguring filter graph because hwaccel changed"); see
  ffmpeg-n9.0.1-keep-graph-on-equivalent-hwframes.patch.
- The decoder thread destroys the context while the filter thread is still
  syncing frames it already received (up to 2 are queued between them,
  DEFAULT_FRAME_THREAD_QUEUE_SIZE); libvmaf_sycl calls vaSyncSurface on each
  one. Zero-copy scores crossing the S05E03 and S05E04 end splices
  segfaulted iHD on fc0 ("segfault at 10", iHD_drv_video.so+0x90a61c), with
  compute (ccs) and blitter (bcs) engine resets.

The crash is a destroy racing a status query on another thread, with no lock
in common (intel-media-26.3.2, media-driver ff3fed1):
- Decoder thread: vaDestroyContext -> DdiDecodeFunctions::DestroyContext ->
  CleanUp (ddi_decode_functions.cpp:377-418, 1300) ->
  DdiDecodeBase::DestroyContext, which runs codecHal->Destroy(), then
  MOS_Delete(codecHal), then clears pCodecHal, and only then each surface's
  pDecCtx (ddi_decode_base_specific.cpp:452, 453, 455, 464). The only lock
  it takes is DecoderMutex, around the context-heap release
  (ddi_decode_functions.cpp:400-403). codecHal->Destroy() reaches
  HevcPipelineXe_Lpm_Plus_Base::Destroy()
  (decode_hevc_pipeline_xe_lpm_plus_base.cpp:352-359) and
  DecodePipeline::Uninitialize(), which waits for the submitted commands and
  then runs MOS_Delete(m_statusReport)
  (decode_pipeline.cpp:275, 282); MOS_Delete sets the pointer to nullptr
  (mos_utilities.h:3132-3149).
- Filter thread: SyncSurface reads pDecCtx with no lock and, while it is
  still set, picks the decode component (media_libva_interface_next.cpp:
  1377-1389). The decode StatusCheck reads pDecCtx again, takes SurfaceMutex,
  reads pCodecHal (ddi_decode_functions.cpp:1020-1023) and goes on to
  StatusReport, which calls decoder->GetCompletedReport()
  (ddi_decode_functions.cpp:1048): the adapter
  (decode_hevc_pipeline_adapter_xe2_hpm.cpp:123-126) -> the pipeline
  (decode_hevc_pipeline_xe_lpm_plus_base.cpp:417-421) ->
  m_statusReport->GetCompletedCount() (media_status_report.h:172-178).
A status query that read pCodecHal before it was cleared and gets to
GetCompletedReport after Uninitialize deleted m_statusReport dereferences
NULL; one that gets there after MOS_Delete(codecHal) is a use-after-free
with no fixed signature.

That this is the faulting instruction is inferred from offsets, not from a
symbolised backtrace. The code at +0x90a61c starts endbr64;
mov 0x58(%rdi),%rax; mov 0x60(%rax),%rax; mov 0x10(%rax),%rdx, and the fault
address is 0x10. In the 26.3.2 headers that is adapter->m_decoder at +0x58
(codechal_common.h:409-425; decode_hevc_pipeline_adapter_xe2_hpm.h:74),
pipeline->m_statusReport at +0x60 (media_pipeline.h:259-268, taking gmmlib's
PLATFORM, not on disk, as 28 or 32 bytes, which both give +0x60) and
m_completedCount at +0x10 (media_status_report.h:247-248): a NULL
m_statusReport. An earlier crash at +0x9a9d5c, captured in an image with the
same iHD build (26.3.2-1~24.04~ppa1, build-id
8bf64c6215d4c43cf96dabb7336eff1e731b54dc), starts with the same
instructions, so it is a second function in the same binary. That fits the
per-codec adapter copies of GetCompletedReport (the AV1 one is
decode_av1_pipeline_adapter_xe2_hpm.cpp:117-121). That crash was the AV1
(distorted) decoder freed at thread exit, which
ffmpeg-n9.0.1-keep-decoder-until-cleanup.patch fixed; these are the HEVC
(reference) decoder re-initialising at the SPS change. A NULL check on
pDecCtx (or decCtx) in iHD would not fix it: both pDecCtx and pCodecHal are
still set when the status query reads them, and the pipeline is torn down
underneath it.

With this patch, hevc_frame_start() decides before set_sps() drops the old SPS
whether the running hwaccel can decode the new SPS as it is. If it can,
get_format() skips ff_get_format(). The VA config, VA context, frames context
and hwaccel private data stay exactly as they were, so there is no destroy to
race, and avctx->pix_fmt is set back to the hwaccel format that
export_stream_params() overwrote (hevcdec.c:339). export_multilayer() and
setup_multilayer() still run. One line is logged at INFO, because production
reads run at -loglevel info and this is their only sign that a change was
kept:
"Keeping hwaccel hevc_vaapi for a new SPS: coded WxH, FMT, profile P unchanged
(packet pts N)".

Kept only when all of these hold (hwaccel_keeps_for_sps()):
- A hwaccel is initialised (avctx->hwaccel, its private data and
  hw_frames_ctx set), and it is VA-API. Other hwaccels were not checked, and
  their state depends on other things: vulkan copies SPS fields, HRD
  included, into its session parameters (vulkan_hevc.c:220-239), videotoolbox
  builds its format description from the parameter sets (videotoolbox.c:874,
  988), and nvdec and dxva2 size fixed surface pools at init (nvdec.c:416,
  441; dxva2.c:620-634).
- There is an old SPS. The first activation, and the one after a failed
  get_format() (hevcdec.c:3262), go the usual way.
- Same coded width and height as the old SPS, and the frames context has that
  size. They set the frames size (vaapi_decode.c:619-621), the
  vaCreateContext size (vaapi_decode.c:715-716) and the hardware limits check
  (vaapi_decode.c:596-607). iHD sizes its HAL settings and bitstream and slice
  buffers from them at context creation (ddi_decode_functions.cpp:174, 227,
  238; ddi_decode_hevc_specific.cpp:928-983, 1180-1181, 1247).
- Same sps->pix_fmt as the old SPS, and it equals avctx->sw_pix_fmt. pix_fmt
  encodes bit depth, chroma format, GBR and, for 8-bit 4:2:0 only, full range
  (ps.c:1190-1216, 971-983). ff_get_format() takes sw_pix_fmt from it
  (decode.c:1247), and the surface fourcc is chosen from sw_pix_fmt
  (vaapi_decode.c:329-390). Bit depth and chroma format also choose the
  VAProfile, from which iHD fixes lumaChromaDepth and chromaFormat for the
  life of the context (ddi_decode_hevc_specific.cpp:1184-1213), and
  DecodeBasicFeature::Init locks m_is10Bit, m_chromaFormat and m_bitDepth from
  those settings (decode_basic_feature.cpp:71-77). They size the HCP buffers
  for every picture (decode_hevc_picture_packet.cpp:233-234), so this
  condition is required, not only prudent. Only an 8-bit 4:2:0 range change
  (YUV420P <-> YUVJ420P, ps.c:971-972) re-initialises. At 10 bits full and
  limited range are both YUV420P10, so a range-only change keeps the hwaccel.
  That is safe for VA-API: its picture parameters have no range field
  (vaapi_hevc.c:146-211), and export_stream_params() still exports
  color_range (hevcdec.c:350-354).
- Same general_profile_idc, and it is Main, Main 10 or Main Still Picture. The
  VAProfile comes from avctx->profile through vaapi_profile_map
  (vaapi_decode.c:520-574), and per-picture buffer sizes depend on it
  (vaapi_hevc.c:234, 301, 354, 468). RExt and SCC always re-initialise: their
  VAProfile is derived from the SPS constraint flags (vaapi_hevc.c:597-657).
- Same VPS object. What get_format() exports and sets up from the VPS
  (layers, alpha, view IDs; hevcdec.c:577-578, 708, 718) is then unchanged. A
  byte-identical VPS repeat keeps its object (ps.c:797-800).

Deliberately not compared:
- The DPB size. It sizes no pool: with libva >= 1 the decoder frames context
  has initial_pool_size 0 (vaapi_decode.c:628-629; decode.c:1105 and 1161 add
  nothing to 0) and its pool grows on demand (hwcontext_vaapi.c:541-542,
  657-665). sps_max_dec_pic_buffering_minus1 goes to the driver with every
  picture (vaapi_hevc.c:150), and iHD's HAL uses it only in a debug dump
  (codechal_debug.cpp:305). It is only uncompared when the VPS is identical:
  the VPS carries its own DPB size, reorder count and latency
  (ps.c:849-851) and must be the same object, so a DPB change that also
  changes the VPS re-initialises. A byte-identical VPS with only the SPS
  values changed is the one case where not comparing it matters.
- Level, tier, HRD, VUI timing, the conformance window, and every coding tool
  or partitioning field. VA-API has no HRD field at all. The rest is filled
  for each picture from the current SPS and PPS (vaapi_hevc.c:146-211;
  transform depths 154-155, transform_skip 181). iHD copies it into its
  picture parameters each time (ddi_decode_hevc_specific.cpp:286-303, 332),
  and the HAL recomputes size and CTB layout per picture
  (decode_hevc_basic_feature.cpp:558-570) and resizes MV buffers on demand
  (decode_hevc_mv_buffers.cpp:57-73).

Why after the change nothing still assumes a re-initialised hwaccel:
- VAAPIDecodeContext holds only the VA IDs and device/frames pointers
  (vaapi_decode.h:47-61), all still valid. No SPS or PPS pointer is cached.
  vaapi_hevc_start_frame() takes h->pps and pps->sps for each picture
  (vaapi_hevc.c:129-133).
- The old SPS stays alive while it is compared: sps_list already holds the new
  one (ps.c:1774-1779), but l->sps still references the old one until
  set_sps() (hevcdec.c:730). DPB frames keep their own PPS reference, and
  through it their SPS (refs.c:193). The keep path stores no SPS pointer.
- The refs are still cleared (hevcdec.c:3251) and the previous sequence is
  still bumped (hevcdec.c:3312). To iHD the IRAP is one more random-access
  picture in a running context.
- H.264 already works this way: it calls get_format() again only when coded
  size, bit depth, chroma format or pixel format change (h264_slice.c:
  1082-1095, 1136, 1154).

Frame threading, which production reads use: the hevc VA-API hwaccel is
ASYNC_SAFE but not THREAD_SAFE (vaapi_hevc.c:672), so one instance of its
state is handed from thread to thread: stashed in ff_thread_finish_setup()
(pthread_frame.c:693-697) and swapped into the next worker when its packet is
submitted (pthread_frame.c:537-543). What serialises the keep test against
the previous worker's use of that state is hwaccel_mutex: a worker holding a
thread-unsafe hwaccel locks it before it decodes, so before
hevc_frame_start() (pthread_frame.c:270-274), and a worker unlocks it only
after its whole decode, slices and end_frame included (pthread_frame.c:
302-312). So the test sees the live hwaccel with nothing running on it. The
worker holds avctx->hwaccel, the private data and the propagated
hw_frames_ctx (pthread_frame.c:384-393). On the keep path nothing is
uninitialised, so the handover is unchanged. pix_fmt and sw_pix_fmt reach the
next thread and the user as before (pthread_frame.c:353-354). Other threads
pick up the SPS without get_format() (hevcdec.c:4028-4032). Worker contexts
have their own in_pkt for the log line (pthread_frame.c:867).

Interaction with the other patches:
- keep-decoder-until-cleanup is still needed: it moves the one remaining
  vaDestroyContext per decoder after the filtergraphs.
- keep-graph-on-equivalent-hwframes is no longer reached for these changes.
  Frames keep the same hw_frames_ctx pointer, so send_frame() sees no hwaccel
  change and no second decoder pool is allocated. It still matters for
  changes this patch re-initialises that end up with an equivalent frames
  context (another VPS or profile_idc with the same size and format), for
  other decoders, and as a guard.

Conditions and limits:
- Real changes still re-initialise, and the destroy-vs-sync race is still
  there for them: a new coded size, bit depth, chroma format, 8-bit range,
  profile or VPS, and any RExt/SCC stream. Such a read still rebuilds its
  graph and logs "Reconfiguring filter graph".
- With keep-decoder-until-cleanup, those changes and get_format() error paths
  are the only destroys left in the middle of a zero-copy read. AV1, the
  distorted input, is not one: at a repeated sequence header
  get_pixel_format() returns before ff_get_format() when avctx->pix_fmt is
  already in its list (av1dec.c:660-664). The other ff_hwaccel_uninit()
  callers are close (avcodec.c:474, pthread_frame.c:769) and
  pthread_frame.c:419 and 443, which only thread-safe hwaccels reach. A sync
  strictly after a destroy has returned is safe only for a surface still in
  that context's render-target table: BeginPicture sets pDecCtx before it
  registers the surface (ddi_decode_base_specific.cpp:265, 281), and a
  surface replaced in an inactive slot keeps a pDecCtx
  (ddi_codec_base_specific.cpp:94-107) that dangles once CleanUp frees the
  context (ddi_decode_functions.cpp:1302). With keep-decoder-until-cleanup
  that last destroy comes after the filtergraphs, so fftools does not reach
  it.
- The user's get_format() callback is not called for a kept change. fftools'
  callback (ffmpeg_dec.c:1313-1353) returns the same format for the same list,
  and its multiview setup returns early unless views were requested
  (ffmpeg_dec.c:1096-1101). hwaccel_flags and extra_hw_frames changed by an
  API user between the two SPS are not re-read.
- hevc_qsv is a separate decoder (qsvdec.c) and is not changed.
- Verified offline only: applies at --fuzz=0 to n9.0.1 on its own and after
  the four other ffmpeg-n9.0.1 patches, and hevcdec.c compiles with no
  warnings. The new code has no #if, so it is compiled even where VA-API is
  not configured. That the keep path is taken at the end splices, removes the
  segfault, and decodes the same frames as software still needs a run on the
  card. Line numbers are n9.0.1's (media-driver: intel-media-26.3.2,
  ff3fed1); another FFMPEG_REF needs the patch re-cut.

--- a/libavcodec/hevc/hevcdec.c
+++ b/libavcodec/hevc/hevcdec.c
@@ -560,7 +560,59 @@
